cartridge "KABLAM!/STEPHEN RESSLER/1985"

' KABLAM!
' A ColecoVision game based on "KABLAM!" by Stephen Ressler, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette, January 1985 (Commodore 64 and VIC-20, BASIC with a
' machine-language core in DATA lines).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: a prankster on the rooftop walks back and forth along the
' ledge and drops firecrackers one at a time. Push your blast bin along the
' pavement and catch each one before it hits the street. Every catch
' scores a point; every ten catches the prankster speeds up. A missed
' firecracker goes KABLAM! and costs a spare bin; miss with no spare left
' and the game is over (four misses in all).
'
' From the original (orig/kablam_64.bas, its machine code disassembled in
' orig/ml.dis): the skill level 1-15 (delay K = (16 - skill) x 15 + 15),
' the bomber and the firecracker both moving one pixel a step with that
' delay, the bomber bouncing between the same edges, the firecracker
' dropped straight down from where the bomber is when the last one was
' caught or burst, a point a catch (seven-digit score), five units less
' delay every ten catches, three spare bins shown in the score line
' (the game ends on the miss after the last spare), the bin's speed of
' 4 pixels a frame and its wrap from one edge to the other, the burst
' freezing the play, and the end: ten bursts over the town, GAME OVER.
'
' Liberties: original art and sound (a raccoon instead of the original's
' figure, a rolling bin instead of its cup); speeds converted from the
' measured C64 step time (about 45 K + 900 cycles plus 15% of interrupt
' time) to 1/16 pixel a frame; the firecracker's fall is shorter on this
' screen (120 lines for 154), so its vertical speed is scaled by 25/32 to
' keep the C64's fall time. The delay stops shrinking at K = 25 (on the
' C64 it went on, past 0, and wrapped round to the slowest speed). The
' score shows "KABLAM!" over the burst and leaves a scorch mark. The
' skill is chosen with the stick on the title instead of typed. The
' original's pause key (the space bar) is left out (joystick only).
'
' Controls: either controller; the stick moves the bin left and right.
' On the title: up/down choose START GAME or CREDITS, left/right the
' skill, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table; the title tune
' is played once. Nothing flashes faster than 3 Hz.

